<h2>What tilts the choice toward Samadhaan</h2>

<p>Three features make the Council route the default for a clean delayed-payment claim. The interest arithmetic transforms settlement dynamics: a buyer facing compound interest at three times the bank rate, accruing monthly, has a burning incentive to settle that no ordinary suit generates. The Section 19 pre-deposit then guards the outcome: a buyer cannot park an adverse award in a Section 34 challenge without depositing 75% of the awarded amount, and courts can release part of that deposit to the supplier pending the challenge. And the forum is purpose-built: the reference is filed online through the Samadhaan portal with invoices and Udyam registration, without court fees on a plaint scale.</p>

<h2>What tilts it toward a suit</h2>

<p>The Council route presupposes a supplier within the Act: micro or small (with the position of medium enterprises for Chapter V benefits requiring care), registered at the time of the transactions, and a claim that is in substance for the price of goods supplied or services rendered. Where the claim travels beyond Section 17 — damages for wrongful termination, loss of profit, disputes about a larger contract of which supply is one element — the suit accommodates what the reference may not. Where registration post-dates the supplies, the statutory benefits are compromised, and pretending otherwise invites years of jurisdictional litigation. And where the supplier itself anticipates substantial counterclaims, the commercial court's two-way procedure, disclosure regime and appellate structure may serve better than statutory arbitration configured around the supplier's dues.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Watch the clock in either lane. The reference and the suit both answer to the Limitation Act, 1963 — three years from when the invoices fell due, subject to acknowledgments extending time. Buyers' part-payments and signed reconciliations are worth securing not only commercially but for the fresh limitation they start.</p>
</div>

<h2>Sequencing and hybrid strategies</h2>

<p>The routes are alternatives for the same amount — double recovery is impermissible — but sequencing has room for craft. A statutory demand notice often precedes either lane and tests the buyer's defences. An operational-creditor route under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 remains available for undisputed debts above the threshold, with its own leverage. A supplier already in a Council reference should resist the buyer's parallel suit fragmenting the dispute, invoking Section 24 MSMED's overriding effect; a supplier suing should plead its MSME status anyway, because Section 16 interest has been applied even outside references where the Act governs the transaction. The constant across strategies is paper: purchase orders, acceptance, invoices, delivery proof and the ledger — the file that wins the fast routes and survives the slow ones.</p>

<p>The chamber of Advocate Manish Jha advises MSMEs and buyers in delayed-payment references, commercial suits and enforcement proceedings in Delhi. This article is for general information; it is not legal advice and does not create an advocate-client relationship.</p>
